Ted Nelson invented the concept of hypertext kind of pre, before there was a web, and worked on a project called Xanadu, which is where a lot of the ideas for the modern web came from. But Xanadu was kind of this nutty system where you had links were bi-directional. So if you created a link to something then you it wasn't just to the thing but the thing created a link to something, then you... it wasn't just to the thing, but the thing that you linked to could also get back. So it was like, you know, built-in Google search into the links. And this was... I don't know, this was maybe 88, 89. Ted Nelson gave this talk on Xanadu, and this was way before the web existed, and talked for probably six hours, you know, so from midnight to 6 a.m. And I thought the man was brilliant, but completely nuts.

